 ## Proof
 
-1.1 For the $n$ in [A1], and any natural frequency $m$ with $1\le m<2n$, put $\alpha:=\iota(m)\pi/\iota(n)$ and $C_m:=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\cos(\iota(j)\alpha)$. Positivity of canonical naturals gives $\iota(n)>0$, and $1\le m<2n$ gives $0<\alpha/2<\pi$; hence $\sin(\alpha/2)>0$ by [F4]. The half-weighted endpoints are the DCT-I convention fixed by [F1]. By [F2], $2\sin(\alpha/2)C_m=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\bigl(\sin((\iota(j)+\tfrac12)\alpha)-\sin((\iota(j)-\tfrac12)\alpha)\bigr)=\sin((\iota(n)-\tfrac12)\alpha)+\sin(\alpha/2)=2\sin(\iota(n)\alpha/2)\cos((\iota(n)-1)\alpha/2).$ Since $\sin(\alpha/2)>0$, this gives $C_m=\sin(\iota(m)\pi/2)\cos((\iota(n)-1)\iota(m)\pi/(2\iota(n)))/\sin(\iota(m)\pi/(2\iota(n)))$. [given, A1, F1, F2, F4, algebra]
+1.1 For the $n$ in [A1], and any natural frequency $m$ with $1\le m<2n$, put $\alpha:=m\pi/n$ and $C_m:=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\cos(j\alpha)$. Positivity of canonical naturals gives $n>0$, and $1\le m<2n$ gives $0<\alpha/2<\pi$; hence $\sin(\alpha/2)>0$ by [F4]. The half-weighted endpoints are the DCT-I convention fixed by [F1]. By [F2], $2\sin(\alpha/2)C_m=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\bigl(\sin((j+\tfrac12)\alpha)-\sin((j-\tfrac12)\alpha)\bigr)=\sin((n-\tfrac12)\alpha)+\sin(\alpha/2)=2\sin(n\alpha/2)\cos((n-1)\alpha/2).$ Since $\sin(\alpha/2)>0$, this gives $C_m=\sin(m\pi/2)\cos((n-1)m\pi/(2n))/\sin(m\pi/(2n))$. [given, A1, F1, F2, F4, algebra]
 
